    "lawCode": "Amd §§83.03 & 83.39, Ment Hyg L",
    "lawSection": "Mental Hygiene Law",
    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:S5482\n\nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend the mental hygiene law, in relation to\nthe effect of registration for purposes of the uniform guardianship\nand protective proceedings jurisdiction act\n\nPURPOSE: To clarify provisions adopted as part of the Uniform Adult\nGuardianship and Protective Proceedings Jurisdiction Act of 2013\nrelating to the right of guardians appointed in other states to appear\nin New York courts and dispose of real estate.\n\nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 amends section 83.03 to make a technical correction by\nadding a comma\n\nSection 2 amends section 83.39 of the mental hygiene law to make it\nclear that an adult guardian appointed in another state, if authorized\nto do so by the appointing court, could appear in New York courts. It\nalso clarifies that all \"protective orders\" not just orders appointing\na guardian of the property or a guardian of the person, are\nenforceable pursuant to this section of law. Protective orders are\nalready defined in section 83.03(i) to mean an order appointing a\nconservator guardian of the property or other order related to\nmanagement of an adult's property, yet protective orders were\ninadvertently left out of section 83.39. Finally, it clarifies that\nadult guardians appointed in another state may use the same procedure\nto obtain authority to sell real property in New York that guardians\nappointed in New York use without first having to go through the\nduplicative process of having to be appointed a guardian in New York.\n\nSection 3 - Effective date.\n\nJUSTIFICATION:  By Chapter 427 of the laws of 2013 New York joined\nsome thirty other states in adopting the Uniform Adult Guardianship\nand Protective Proceedings Jurisdiction Act (UAGPPJA. The law, which\nbecame effective on April 21, 2014, establishes a uniform set of rules\nfor determining jurisdiction, and thus, simplifies the process for\ndetermining jurisdiction between multiple states in adult guardianship\ncases. It also establishes a framework that allows state court judges\nin different states to communicate with each other about adult\nguardianship cases. By adopting the UAGPPJA, the New York State court\nsystem avoids costly duplicative guardianship cases and people are\nbetter served.\n\nThis legislation makes minor, technical clarifying amendments to New\nYork State's UAGPPJA which reflect the original intent. It makes clear\nthat an adult guardian appointed in another state, if authorized to do\nso by the appointing court, can appear in New York courts and is\nauthorized to sell real estate, using the same process as NY appointed\nguardians, without having to go through a duplicative NY guardianship\nprocess.\n\nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY: New bill.\n\nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:  None.\n\n\nEFFECTIVE DATE: This act shall take effect immediately.\n\n",
